# nats-server.conf — generated from nats-server.conf.template by nats-config-gen.
# ${NATS_BRIDGE_NKEY_PUB}, ${NATS_ALICE_PASSWORD}, ${NATS_BOB_PASSWORD},
# ${NATS_JOHN_PASSWORD}, ${NATS_MARY_PASSWORD} and ${OPEN_TENANT} are substituted at
# startup (nats-config-gen, from .env.admin).
#
# ⚠️ Tenant stream names are the tenant token VERBATIM: CDC_acme, not CDC_ACME.
#
# They used to be uppercased here because nats-init created them with
# `tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'`. f3be486 moved stream creation into the BRIDGE, which
# builds `<cdc_streams.tenant_prefix><tenant>` from grammar.json and the roster — so
# the bridge makes CDC_acme while this file granted CDC_ACME, and a client with a
# perfectly good tenant was refused its own stream. Measured 2026-08-28. The operator
# side never had the bug: it grants `CDC_{{tag(tenant)}}`, verbatim. Note that the
# subjects in this file (`cdc.acme.>`) and the object store (`OBJ_gen-acme`) were
# always lowercase — only the stream name diverged.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file is where PROTOCOL.md §7.1 stops being a claim and becomes a fact.
#
# The protocol says a mutation's principal is trustworthy because it is a *subject
# token*, and NATS authorises subjects: a client granted `publish: ["mutation.alice.>"]`
# cannot write as bob no matter what it puts in its payload. That guarantee lives
# entirely in this file. Delete the permissions blocks below and the principal
# degrades to a self-asserted string — the "trust me, I am alice" the design exists
# to replace. The bridge cannot detect the difference and does not try to.
#
# Two credential shapes, on purpose:
#
#   the bridge  — nkey, seed passed on the command line, never written to any env
#                 file. It is the trusted writer: it holds PostgreSQL replication
#                 rights already, so restricting its subjects buys little, while a
#                 wrong entry breaks every table at once and silently.
#   a client    — user/password, one per principal, allow-listed to its own
#                 mutation subtree and to read-only access on everything else.
#
# ⚠️ A password shipped to a *browser* is not a secret. Anyone with devtools reads
# it out of the bundle, and can then write as that principal from curl. This block
# makes identity **enforced** (NATS checks it) without making it **secret** (the
# holder is not proven to be the person). For server-side consumers — the Go and
# Python clients — that gap does not exist and user/password is the right answer.
# For browsers the endpoint is short-lived credentials minted per session by your
# own auth server; the permissions below are the same either way, so this is the
# right shape to build against now.
#
# ── The migration path, so this file is not a dead end ───────────────────────
#
# A user entry per principal does not scale, and it is not meant to: the endpoint
# is operator/JWT mode with a **scoped signing key**, where the permission shape
# below is written *once* and every user inherits it.
#
#   nsc edit signing-key --account APP --role client --sk A… \
#     --allow-pub "mutation.{{name()}}.>" \
#     --allow-sub "cdc.>" --allow-sub "init.>" --allow-sub "_INBOX.>"
#
# `{{name()}}` expands at user-creation time, so the login ID your auth server
# already has *becomes* the principal — which is the whole trick. After login it
# mints a short-lived user JWT named with that ID, signed by the signing key, and
# hands it to the browser with an ephemeral user nkey whose seed never leaves the
# page. No server config changes when a user is added, ever.
#
# Two properties worth knowing before that day:
#
#  - Permissions come from the **signing key's scope**, not from the user JWT, so
#    a compromised minting service cannot widen them — only name a user.
#  - Editing the scope propagates to every connected user immediately.
#
# The subject grammar is what makes this work: because the principal is a token
# (§7.1) and not a payload field, one wildcard covers a user for all tables and
# adding a table reissues no credentials. The allow-lists below are the same
# lists, so nothing here is throwaway.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

port: 4222
http_port: 8222

# Every client's read side is identical — they all follow the same replica — so it
# is named once. Only the *write* side differs per principal, which is the point.
#
# `_INBOX.>` is not optional and its absence is a nasty failure: JetStream requests
# (publishing to a stream, creating a consumer, fetching a batch) are request/reply,
# and without an inbox subscription the reply is dropped by the server and the client
# hangs until its own timeout with no error saying why.
# ⚠️ Spelled out **per principal**, not shared, because one entry is per-principal:
# `mutation_ack.alice.>` is alice's own write verdicts, and pointing every client at
# `mutation_ack.>` would let each read the others' failures — including the SQL detail of
# rows they never wrote.
#
# The shared part therefore has to be repeated for every user added here. That is not an
# oversight: NATS config will not splice a variable into a list (`allow: [$BASE, "x"]`
# fails with *"Subject in permissions array cannot be cast to string"*), so static config
# has no way to express "the common set, plus this". It is exactly the duplication that
# `--allow-sub "…{{name()}}…"` on a scoped signing key removes — see the migration note
# at the top of this file.
# ⚠️ `cdc.acme.>`, NOT `cdc.>`. A principal granted the whole feed is separated from other
# tenants only by the `filter_subject` its own client chooses — which is not enforcement, it
# is etiquette. Measured before this changed: alice, tenant `acme`, subscribed to
# `cdc.globex.>` with these very credentials and received `cdc.globex.test_types.insert`.
#
# ⚠️ `cdc.users.>` is a SECOND entry and not an oversight. Tables with no tenant column
# publish on the bare `cdc.<table>.<op>` subject (they are recorded in
# `zebridge_catalogue` (public rows; the bridge reconciles CDC_PUBLIC's subjects from it at boot), so a tenant grant
# alone cannot reach them. Add a public table, add a line here — and to the matching
# `filter_subjects` in the client, or it subscribes to something it is not granted and looks
# idle rather than refused.
#
# ⚠️ `cdc.${OPEN_TENANT}.>` is the THIRD: the open tenant, the value a tenant column takes
# when a writer left it NULL or empty (`zebridge_guard_tenant()`). Those rows are nobody's
# and everybody's — they publish under a token no declared tenant owns, and `nats-init`
# binds that token into the CDC_PUBLIC stream alongside the public tables. Same value,
# one env (`OPEN_TENANT` in .env.admin) rendered into the guard, the stream and here.
ALICE_READ = [
  "cdc.acme.>"                 # her tenant's change feed — and nobody else's
  "cdc.users.>"                # public tables, which carry no tenant token
  "cdc.orders.>"               # public FK-demo table (PROTOCOL.md §4)
  "cdc.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"       # the open tenant: rows that opted into no tenant
  "mutation_ack.alice.>"       # her own write verdicts, keyed by her Nats-Msg-Id
  "$KV.schemas.>"              # schema descriptors
  "$KV.generations.>"        # generation chain manifests (NOTES.md §1.13)
  "_INBOX.>"                   # JetStream replies — omit this and everything hangs
]

# ⚠️ A separate list, and the ONE line that differs is the one that matters.
#
# Copying a principal's block and reusing the original's read variable is the trap this
# whole comment exists for: bob would then be granted `mutation_ack.alice.>` — able to read
# **her** verdicts, including SQL detail for rows he never wrote — while his own verdicts
# land on a subject he may not subscribe to. Writes still succeed, so nothing looks broken;
# the client simply never receives an answer and, following §7.1, retries forever.
#
# It is a leak and a hang from a single copy-paste. Add a principal, add a list.
BOB_READ = [
  "cdc.globex.>"               # HIS tenant — the same one-line difference as mutation_ack
  "cdc.users.>"                # public tables
  "cdc.orders.>"               # public FK-demo table (PROTOCOL.md §4)
  "cdc.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"       # the open tenant — same subject as alice's, by design
  "mutation_ack.bob.>"         # HIS own verdicts — not alice's
  "$KV.schemas.>"
  "$KV.generations.>"        # generation chain manifests (NOTES.md §1.13)
  "_INBOX.>"
]

# ⚠️ `john` has NO `cdc.<tenant>.>` line — he is mapped to OPEN_TENANT in
# `zebridge_user_tenants`, not to a private tenant, so there is no tenant stream for him
# to read. Public tables plus the open tenant are everything he is entitled to.
JOHN_READ = [
  "cdc.users.>"                # public tables
  "cdc.orders.>"               # public FK-demo table (PROTOCOL.md §4)
  "cdc.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"       # HIS tenant, in the open-mapping sense — same subject as
                                # alice's and bob's open-tenant reads, by design
  "mutation_ack.john.>"        # HIS own verdicts
  "$KV.schemas.>"
  "$KV.generations.>"        # generation chain manifests (NOTES.md §1.13)
  "_INBOX.>"
]

# ⚠️ Also `globex`, like bob. Two principals, one tenant — exercises the KV.tenants
# bucket resolving the same value from two independent zebridge_user_tenants rows,
# not a shared credential.
MARY_READ = [
  "cdc.globex.>"
  "cdc.users.>"
  "cdc.orders.>"               # public FK-demo table (PROTOCOL.md §4)
  "cdc.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
  "mutation_ack.mary.>"        # HER own verdicts — not bob's
  "$KV.schemas.>"
  "$KV.generations.>"        # generation chain manifests (NOTES.md §1.13)
  "_INBOX.>"
]

# ⚠️ Also `globex`, like bob and mary. A third principal on the same tenant — same
# reasoning as mary's own comment: `$KV.tenants.nina` is its own key, resolving the
# same value from its own `zebridge_user_tenants` row, not a shared credential.
NINA_READ = [
  "cdc.globex.>"
  "cdc.users.>"
  "cdc.orders.>"               # public FK-demo table (PROTOCOL.md §4)
  "cdc.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
  "mutation_ack.nina.>"        # HER own verdicts — not bob's or mary's
  "$KV.schemas.>"
  "$KV.generations.>"        # generation chain manifests (NOTES.md §1.13)
  "_INBOX.>"
]

authorization {

  # No `default_permissions`: an unlisted user should be able to do nothing, and a
  # default here would silently grant reach to a user whose permissions block was
  # mistyped or forgotten.

  BRIDGE = { publish = ">", subscribe = ">" }

  users: [
    { nkey: "${NATS_BRIDGE_NKEY_PUB}", permissions: $BRIDGE }

    # ── one entry per principal ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Store the password bcrypted rather than in clear: `nats server passwd`
    # prints a `$2a$…` hash that goes here verbatim.
    { user: "alice", password: "${NATS_ALICE_PASSWORD}", permissions: {
        publish = { allow: [
          # The only line that differs between principals, and the only line that
          # matters for integrity: alice's writes are confined to her own subtree.
          "mutation.alice.>"

          # stream, so a client may ask for any table's snapshot — asking is not
          # reading, and the reply lands on init.> which it may already see.

          # The account-info probe. `jetstreamManager(nc)` issues this before anything
          # else, so omitting it does not fail one feature — it fails the connection,
          # with `Permissions Violation for Publish to "$JS.API.INFO"` arriving *after*
          # a successful "Connected" line. It reveals limits and usage for the account,
          # nothing about other principals' data.
          "$JS.API.INFO"

          # JetStream plumbing, scoped to the streams a client is allowed to read.
          # `CREATE.<STREAM>` and `CREATE.<STREAM>.>` are both needed: in NATS `X.>`
          # does not match `X` itself, and an ephemeral consumer is created on
          # the bare form.
          #
          # Scoping to named streams is what stops alice creating a consumer on
          # MUTATIONS and reading everyone else's writes — `$JS.API.CONSUMER.>`
          # would hand her exactly that, which is why it is not used here.
          # ⚠️ **The stream name is the read boundary, not the subject.** A JetStream
          # consumer never subscribes to the subject it filters on, so `cdc.acme.>` in the
          # subscribe list below cannot stop a client creating a consumer on a shared stream
          # filtered at another tenant. Measured: alice was refused a core SUB to
          # `cdc.globex.>` and then pulled globex rows through a consumer on the wide CDC
          # stream, same credentials. `scripts/scenarios/crosstenant.py` pins it.
          #
          # Naming the streams here is therefore what actually enforces the tenant split.
          # CDC_PUBLIC carries tables with no tenant column and is granted to everyone.
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_acme",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_acme.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_PUBLIC",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_schemas",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_schemas.>"
          # ── Generations (NOTES.md §1.13): chain manifests + per-tenant object buckets.
          # KV_generations keys are `<tenant>.<table>`, so DIRECT.GET scopes per tenant
          # list; $JS.API.STREAM.NAMES is deliberately absent — bind the stream
          # explicitly (nats.zig can; granting NAMES leaks the stream roster).
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_generations", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_generations"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_generations.$KV.generations.acme.>"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_generations.$KV.generations.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.OBJ_gen-acme"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.OBJ_gen-acme.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.OBJ_gen-acme"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-acme", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-acme.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.OBJ_gen-acme.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.OBJ_gen-acme.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          # `js.consumers.get(stream, name)` reads the consumer back before binding, so
          # this is needed on every stream the client pulls from. Read-only metadata on
          # a stream it may already read.
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.CDC_acme.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.KV_schemas.>"

          # ⚠️ `$JS.API.CONSUMER.DELETE.*` is deliberately absent. The client never
          # deletes a consumer (its ephemerals expire on their inactivity threshold),
          # and granting it on INIT/CDC would let alice delete *bob's* in-flight
          # consumer mid-replay — static config has no name templating, so the grant
          # cannot be narrowed to her own. The bridge's own durables are on MUTATIONS
          # and REQUESTS, which no client can reach at all.

          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.CDC_acme.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.KV_schemas.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.CDC_acme"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.CDC_PUBLIC"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_schemas"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_tenants"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_schemas.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.KV_schemas"

          # ⚠️ PROTOCOL.md Step 0: alice may fetch ONLY her own key. Direct Get puts
          # the key in the SUBJECT, which is what makes this scopable at all — unlike
          # (NOTES.md §1.12 part 3: leaking one principal's tenant must not disclose
          # the roster).
          #
          # ⚠️ Measured, not guessed: the subject is
          # `$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_tenants.$KV.tenants.<key>` — the KV bucket's own
          # internal subject (`$KV.<bucket>.<key>`) as the suffix, not the bare key.
          # A first attempt granting just `...KV_tenants.alice` was refused with
          # `Publish Violation - Subject "...KV_tenants.$KV.tenants.alice"` in the
          # server log — that mismatch is what fixed the line below.
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_tenants.$KV.tenants.alice"

          # Deliberately absent, and not an oversight:
          #   $JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.KV_tenants   — selects the key from the request
          #     BODY, not the subject, so NATS cannot scope it per-key. Granting this
          #     would let alice fetch bob's tenant by asking for his key in the body.
          #   $JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE/INFO/MSG.NEXT.KV_tenants — a consumer reads the
          #     whole stream regardless of key, the same "stream is the read boundary"
          #     problem §1.8/§1.12 describe for CDC.
          # Confirmed empirically, not assumed: with only the exact Direct Get grant
          # above, `nats kv get` never attempted a consumer fallback — one Publish
          # Violation on the exact subject, then the client's own request timeout. No
          # consumer grant is needed for a plain `kv.get()`.
          "$JS.ACK.>"
        ] }
        subscribe = { allow: $ALICE_READ }
    } }

    # ── a second principal, for two-client demos and tenant isolation ──────────
    #
    # ⚠️ Duplicated rather than factored. NATS will not splice a variable into a
    # permissions list (`allow: [$BASE, "extra"]` is not valid), which is noted above —
    # so every principal repeats the whole block, and exactly two lines differ:
    #
    #     publish   "mutation.bob.>"       its own writes, and no one else's
    #     subscribe "mutation_ack.bob.>"   its own verdicts, keyed by its Nats-Msg-Id
    #
    # ⚠️ Getting the SUBSCRIBE line wrong is the quiet failure: writes still work, and
    # every verdict silently goes to a subject this user may not read — so a client
    # following §7.1 can never pop its outbox and retries forever.
    { user: "bob", password: "${NATS_BOB_PASSWORD}", permissions: {
        publish = { allow: [
          # The only line that differs between principals, and the only line that
          # matters for integrity: bob's writes are confined to her own subtree.
          "mutation.bob.>"

          # stream, so a client may ask for any table's snapshot — asking is not
          # reading, and the reply lands on init.> which it may already see.

          # The account-info probe. `jetstreamManager(nc)` issues this before anything
          # else, so omitting it does not fail one feature — it fails the connection,
          # with `Permissions Violation for Publish to "$JS.API.INFO"` arriving *after*
          # a successful "Connected" line. It reveals limits and usage for the account,
          # nothing about other principals' data.
          "$JS.API.INFO"

          # JetStream plumbing, scoped to the streams a client is allowed to read.
          # `CREATE.<STREAM>` and `CREATE.<STREAM>.>` are both needed: in NATS `X.>`
          # does not match `X` itself, and an ephemeral consumer is created on
          # the bare form.
          #
          # Scoping to named streams is what stops bob creating a consumer on
          # MUTATIONS and reading everyone else's writes — `$JS.API.CONSUMER.>`
          # would hand her exactly that, which is why it is not used here.
          # ⚠️ **The stream name is the read boundary, not the subject.** A JetStream
          # consumer never subscribes to the subject it filters on, so `cdc.acme.>` in the
          # subscribe list below cannot stop a client creating a consumer on a shared stream
          # filtered at another tenant. Measured: alice was refused a core SUB to
          # `cdc.globex.>` and then pulled globex rows through a consumer on the wide CDC
          # stream, same credentials. `scripts/scenarios/crosstenant.py` pins it.
          #
          # Naming the streams here is therefore what actually enforces the tenant split.
          # CDC_PUBLIC carries tables with no tenant column and is granted to everyone.
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_globex",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_globex.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_PUBLIC",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_schemas",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_schemas.>"
          # ── Generations (NOTES.md §1.13): chain manifests + per-tenant object buckets.
          # KV_generations keys are `<tenant>.<table>`, so DIRECT.GET scopes per tenant
          # list; $JS.API.STREAM.NAMES is deliberately absent — bind the stream
          # explicitly (nats.zig can; granting NAMES leaks the stream roster).
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_generations", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_generations"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_generations.$KV.generations.globex.>"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_generations.$KV.generations.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.OBJ_gen-globex"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.OBJ_gen-globex.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.OBJ_gen-globex"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-globex", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-globex.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.OBJ_gen-globex.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.OBJ_gen-globex.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          # `js.consumers.get(stream, name)` reads the consumer back before binding, so
          # this is needed on every stream the client pulls from. Read-only metadata on
          # a stream it may already read.
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.CDC_globex.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.KV_schemas.>"

          # ⚠️ `$JS.API.CONSUMER.DELETE.*` is deliberately absent. The client never
          # deletes a consumer (its ephemerals expire on their inactivity threshold),
          # and granting it on INIT/CDC would let bob delete *bob's* in-flight
          # consumer mid-replay — static config has no name templating, so the grant
          # cannot be narrowed to her own. The bridge's own durables are on MUTATIONS
          # and REQUESTS, which no client can reach at all.

          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.CDC_globex.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.KV_schemas.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.CDC_globex"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.CDC_PUBLIC"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_schemas"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_tenants"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_schemas.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.KV_schemas"

          # See alice's block above for why this is the exact key and not a wildcard,
          # and why STREAM.MSG.GET.KV_tenants / CONSUMER.*.KV_tenants are absent.
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_tenants.$KV.tenants.bob"
          "$JS.ACK.>"
        ] }
        subscribe = { allow: $BOB_READ }
    } }

    # ── a third principal, public-only — no tenant, no tenant stream ───────────
    #
    # `john` is mapped to OPEN_TENANT (`_default`) in `zebridge_user_tenants`, not to a
    # private tenant. That is a deliberate membership, not an absence of one — see
    # `init.write.template.sql`'s `zebridge_guard_tenant()`: an *unmapped* principal
    # omitting the tenant column on a write is refused (`check_violation`), because
    # `_default` stopped being a coalesce fallback and became a mapping like any other.
    # Being IN this file at all (a NATS user exists) is a separate gate from being
    # mapped in Postgres — one without the other still cannot do anything: no NATS
    # user means no connection at all, and no Postgres mapping means an omitted-column
    # write is refused even once connected.
    #
    # No `$JS.API.CONSUMER.*.CDC_john` grants below, because there is no such stream —
    # he has no private tenant to have one. `CDC_PUBLIC` and the open-tenant subject
    # are the whole of what he can read, same mechanism as alice's and bob's public
    # reads, just without a second stream alongside it.
    { user: "john", password: "${NATS_JOHN_PASSWORD}", permissions: {
        publish = { allow: [
          "mutation.john.>"


          "$JS.API.INFO"

          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_PUBLIC",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.CDC_PUBLIC.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_schemas",     "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_schemas.>"
          # ── Generations (NOTES.md §1.13): chain manifests + per-tenant object buckets.
          # KV_generations keys are `<tenant>.<table>`, so DIRECT.GET scopes per tenant
          # list; $JS.API.STREAM.NAMES is deliberately absent — bind the stream
          # explicitly (nats.zig can; granting NAMES leaks the stream roster).
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_generations", "$JS.API.CONSUMER.CREATE.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.INFO.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.CONSUMER.MSG.NEXT.KV_generations.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.KV_generations"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.KV_generations.$KV.generations.${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
          "$JS.API.STREAM.INFO.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}"
          "$JS.API.DIRECT.GET.OBJ_gen-${OPEN_TENANT}.>"
